    "result_text": "The earlier failure was just stale compiled artifacts from before the rename; a `clean` build is green: **Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Response to the review\n\nI agree with the finding and addressed it by reconciling the naming rather than leaving the incoherence in place. On the substance:\n\n**The `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity` change is a genuine prerequisite, kept and now made explicit.** The acceptance test creates two owners in the same household (same lastName+postcode, different telephone/email) via plain `POST` and expects both to succeed \u2014 that's the only way a household can hold members with levels for the ceiling to mean anything. The household-only hard rule rejected the second with 409, so it had to be relaxed. It couldn't be split into a separate diff here (single working tree, one task), so instead I made the coupling explicit in the code: `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity`'s javadoc now states the identity keying is what \"makes the household membership-level ceiling meaningful.\" Worth noting the household-only rule was also already internally inconsistent \u2014 `AssignPossibleDuplicate` soft-flags \"same lastName+postcode, different telephone\" matches that the old hard rule could never have let through.\n\n**Reconciled the naming smell you flagged.** Since the check is now identity-keyed, I renamed the exception and its collaborators so the payload and name describe the actual rule:\n- `DuplicateHouseholdException` \u2192 `DuplicateOwnerException`, carrying `identityKey` (not `householdId`), message \"An owner with the same identity already exists\".\n- `DuplicateHouseholdExceptionHandler` \u2192 `DuplicateOwnerExceptionHandler`; the 409 problem-detail now reports `field: identityKey` / `rejectedValue: <identityKey>`.\n- Renamed the escalation YAML to the new FQCN so OfficeFloor still routes it; `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity` throws `DuplicateOwnerException(identity)`.\n- Fixed two now-stale javadocs that described the old household-only rule (`OwnerIdentity`, `AssignPossibleDuplicate`).\n\nThe class name `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity` already matched the new behaviour, so it stayed. No acceptance behaviour changed (the test asserts creates succeed and levels are capped; it doesn't assert the 409 body), and the membership-cap pieces you approved are untouched.",
