    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe membership-cap feature itself is structured well \u2014 my concern is one unrelated change bundled into the diff.\n\n**What's clean (no change needed):**\n- `CapMembershipLevel` is a proper single-responsibility unit: it reads the derived level, delegates the aggregation to `OwnerHousehold`, and applies the ceiling. No logic that belongs elsewhere.\n- `OwnerHousehold.maxMembershipLevel` is placed exactly where it belongs \u2014 right next to `memberCount`, with the identical `(householdId, Iterable<Owner>)` signature and the same \"null householdId matches nothing\" convention. It reuses the household-identity home rather than re-deriving matching logic in the function class. This is the idiomatic move and avoids duplicating the `findAll()`-scan-and-match pattern.\n- YAML wiring is inserted correctly (after `assignMembership`, before `assignMembershipNumber`), carries `govern: [ transaction ]` like its neighbors, and the comment explains the ordering rationale. `service` method name matches the codebase convention.\n\n**Finding \u2014 scope bundling in `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity`:**\nThe diff rewrites the hard-duplicate rule from a household-id match to the `OwnerIdentity` (`telephone|email|householdId`) composite \u2014 a substantive behavioral change to duplicate detection. Nothing in the membership-level spec calls for this; it's a separate concern riding along in a diff whose stated purpose is capping `membershipLevel`.\n\n- A maintainer reading the history for \"cap membership level\" would not expect the duplicate-rejection semantics to change here, and would struggle to attribute/revert either concern independently.\n- If relaxing the duplicate rule is genuinely a prerequisite (so multiple household members with levels can coexist and the cap becomes meaningful), that coupling should be made explicit and justified \u2014 ideally as its own change, or at minimum called out \u2014 rather than folded in silently.\n- Note the residual mismatch this introduces: the class is still named `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity` throwing `DuplicateHouseholdException` with `owner.getHouseholdId()` as its argument, even though the check is no longer household-keyed. The exception payload/name now describes the old rule, not the new identity-based one \u2014 a naming coherence smell left behind by the in-place rewrite.\n\nRecommend splitting the `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity` change out of this diff (or explicitly justifying it as a prerequisite and reconciling the exception naming). The membership-cap pieces can land as-is.",
